{"result":"*******************************************************************************\n Revised: Sep 13, 2021     Spitzer Space Telescope (SST) / (Sun)            -79 \n                              http://spitzer.caltech.edu/\n\n NOTE:\n  - The final Spitzer science observations began 2020-Jan-28\n  - On 2020-Jan-30, the mission was completed, the spacecraft decommissioned \n     and placed in a permanent storage mode.\n  - The spacecraft will drift in its Earth trailing orbit until eventually \n     returning to the vicinity of Earth in ~2051.\n\n Spitzer Space Telescope \n  Launched: Aug 25, 2003\n  Renamed : Dec 18, 2003 (formerly \"SIRTF\": Space Infrared Telescope Facility)\n\n EXTENDED MISSION (2009-present):\n  Cryogen depletion occurred 2009-May-15. A 2-year extended \"warm\" mission \n  will continue operating the 3.6 and 4.5 micron channels on the IRAC \n  instrument with expected sensitivity unchanged from performance in the \n  cryogenic mission. The longer wavelength IRAC channels (5.6 and 8.0 microns) \n  and the MIPS and IRS instruments will be unavailable.\n\n PRIME MISSION (2003-2009):\n  Spitzer will obtain images and spectra by detecting infrared (heat) energy, \n  having wavelengths of 3 and 180 microns, radiated by objects in space.  Most\n  of this infrared radiation is blocked by the Earth's atmosphere and cannot be\n  observed from the ground.  The telescope must be cooled to near absolute zero\n  (-459 degrees F, or -273 degrees Celsius) to record infrared from distant \n  regions of space without interference from the telescope's own heat.\n\n SPACECRAFT:\n  0.85-meter telescope \n  3 cryogenically-cooled science instruments\n  2 solar panels composed of 392 solar cells, producing 427 Watts. \n  Each solar cell is 5.5 x 6.5 cm. Each panel is 50% covered by solar cells,\n   and 50% covered by optical reflectors to reduce panel temp to 330 K.\n  Spitzer cannot point more than 120 degrees away from the sun and maintain\n   adequate power levels.\n\n SPACECRAFT TRAJECTORY:\n   Trajectory was created 2020-Feb-07 11:45:41 and is based on 2-way \n   Doppler tracking data available up until the spacecraft was placed in \n   final safe mode at 2020-Jan-30 22:30 UTC. The trajectory after that \n   is a nominal long-term prediction.\n*******************************************************************************\n\n\n*******************************************************************************\nEphemeris / API_USER Tue Oct 27 09:16:05 2026 Pasadena, USA      / Horizons\n*******************************************************************************\nTarget body name: Spitzer Space Telescope (spacecraft) (-79) {source: SPITZER_merge_2003-2120}\nCenter body name: Earth (399)                     {source: SPITZER_merge_2003-2120}\nCenter-site name: BODY CENTER\n*******************************************************************************\nStart time      : A.D. 2003-Aug-25 12:00:00.0000 UT \nStop  time      : A.D. 2003-Aug-26 12:00:00.0000 UT \nStep-size       : 10 minutes\n*******************************************************************************\nCenter geodetic : 0.0, 0.0, 0.0                   {E-lon(deg),Lat(deg),Alt(km)}\nCenter cylindric: 0.0, 0.0, 0.0                   {E-lon(deg),Dxy(km),Dz(km)}\nCenter radii    : 6378.137, 6378.137, 6356.752 km {Equator_a, b, pole_c}\nOutput units    : KM-S\nCalendar mode   : Mixed Julian/Gregorian\nOutput type     : GEOMETRIC cartesian states\nOutput format   : 2 (position and velocity)\nEOP file        : eop.261026.p270122\nEOP coverage    : DATA-BASED 1962-JAN-20 TO 2026-OCT-26. PREDICTS-> 2027-JAN-21\nReference frame : ICRF\n*******************************************************************************\n            JDUT ,            Calendar Date (UT ),                      X,                      Y,                      Z,                     VX,                     VY,                     VZ,\n**********************************************************